hypotheses.statement:
A two-step path has an interval family with endpoint cuts $Z_1,M_{02}$. A three-step path has a pentagon family with faces $Z_1,Z_2,M_{02},M_{13},M_{03}$. A four-step path has the corresponding three-dimensional associahedral family. Each face carries the product families on its split pieces, and compatible simultaneous cuts are exactly the disjoint noncrossing arc systems.*


*Two consecutive final handles may instead be retained as a fixed segment. For the path $2,\infty,0,1,2$, this gives a pentagon with faces $$\label{eq:ordinary:compressed-cycle}
 Z_1,\quad Z_2,\quad M_{02},\quad M_{04},\quad M_{14}$$ in cyclic order. The $M_{04}$ edge has endpoints $M_{02}$ and $M_{14}$; no cut internal to the fixed segment is introduced.

outputs.exact_statement:
Put $E=-p_1(\operatorname{ad}E)/4$, interpreted by Chern–Weil on the completed connection. Increasing relative charge by one increases $i$ by eight. If a connected piece has $b_1=0$ and only central rational ends, then $$\label{eq:ordinary:central-index}
 i=8E-3(1+b^+).$$ For replacements with identical boundary flats and fixed transitions, including all matching terms, the index difference is $$\label{eq:ordinary:excision}
 \Delta i=8\Delta E-\frac32\Delta(\chi+\sigma).$$ The nonnegative lower charge bounds used below persist for sufficiently small perturbations. On a product neck with a bounded Floer functional perturbation, the required error is bounded independently of the neck length.

A two-step path has an interval family with endpoint cuts $Z_1,M_{02}$. A three-step path has a pentagon family with faces $Z_1,Z_2,M_{02},M_{13},M_{03}$. A four-step path has the corresponding three-dimensional associahedral family. Each face carries the product families on its split pieces, and compatible simultaneous cuts are exactly the disjoint noncrossing arc systems.*


*Two consecutive final handles may instead be retained as a fixed segment. For the path $2,\infty,0,1,2$, this gives a pentagon with faces $$\label{eq:ordinary:compressed-cycle}
 Z_1,\quad Z_2,\quad M_{02},\quad M_{04},\quad M_{14}$$ in cyclic order. The $M_{04}$ edge has endpoints $M_{02}$ and $M_{14}$; no cut internal to the fixed segment is introduced.

Give $L=S^1\times S^2$ the product metric, and let $\alpha$ be a flat connection with parallel stabilizer $H$. On a sufficiently small-energy neck, modulo gauge, the spatial field has the form $$\label{eq:ordinary:harmonic-slice}
 a(t)=\alpha+h(t)\vartheta+b(t),
 \quad h(t)\in H^0_\alpha(L;\operatorname{ad}),
 \quad b(t)\perp H^1_\alpha(L;\operatorname{ad}),
 \quad d_\alpha^*b(t)=0.$$ Here $\vartheta$ is the circle one-form and the temporal component has zero parallel part. For $[-T,T]$ and any sufficiently small fixed decay rate $\delta>0$, the normal part satisfies estimates of the form $$\label{eq:ordinary:normal-decay}
 \|b(t)\|_{k}
 \le C_k\bigl(B_-e^{-\delta(t+T)}
                  +B_+e^{-\delta(T-t)}\bigr)
       +C_k\int_{-T}^T e^{-\delta|t-s|}\rho_k(s)\,ds,$$ where $B_\pm$ measure the end-strip normal data and $\rho_k$ is the decreasing perturbation or background error. The derivative of $h$ is bounded by the same normal size and error. Its total drift is therefore bounded independently of $T$, and on middle strips the field is exponentially close to a single, possibly varying, middle flat.*

*These assertions include a central $\alpha$, in whose fixed slice $h$ has three real coordinates. They give matching up to $H$, exponential convergence on completed ends, and convergence of holonomies uniformly over compact smooth families of bounded paths on the relevant strips. At isolated lens flats the same assertions hold with no harmonic spatial term.

Fix an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism $\phi:Y_{-2}\longrightarrow Y_2$, with fixed bundle and collar identifications. The stacked cobordism and its rigid count $$\label{eq:ordinary:bridge}
 T=f_-\,\phi^{-1}\,f_+:Y_0\longrightarrow Y_0$$ define an integral chain map inducing a unit over $\mathbb F_2$ and an isomorphism over $\mathbb Q$. The cobordism has $$\label{eq:ordinary:bridge-topology}
 H_1(T;\mathbb Z)=0,\qquad \chi(T)=4,\qquad \sigma(T)=-2.$$ Its two increasing-handle halves are simply connected.

At index $i_{\mathrm{total}}=-2$ the face $P$ consists of isolated regular exterior matches at the selected center with a flat $N_U$. Their count induces a mod-two isomorphism. It is also the count on $W'$ over $P$ at index $i'=1$, with the degree-three condition $$\label{eq:negative:beta-cut}
 \operatorname{Hol}_0(a,\beta)=-\operatorname{id},
 \qquad \beta=S^1\times\{0\}\subset D,$$ when the $D$–exterior neck is sufficiently long and finite.

There is a symplectic closure $X_0$ of $Y_0$ such that its two halves are simply connected, $A$ is symplectic, and each half contains a tight genus-two surface $B$. The closure can be chosen to satisfy $$\label{eq:caps:geography}
 q(X_0):=\frac{\chi(X_0)+\sigma(X_0)}4\ge3,
 \qquad s(X_0):=2\chi(X_0)+3\sigma(X_0)\ge q(X_0)-2.$$ Moreover, $[A]$ is primitive and there is $w\in H^2(X_0;\mathbb Z)$ with $\langle w,A\rangle=1$.

For the integral determinant $w$ of Lemma <a href="#lem:caps:closure" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:caps:closure">33</a>, there are $m,a\ge0$ and $h\in H_2(X_0;\mathbb Z)$ such that $$\label{eq:caps:initial-invariant}
       h\cdot A=1,
       \qquad D^w_{X_0}(x_0^m h^a)\ne0.$$

Let $V_g$ be the full determinant-one instanton homology over $\mathbb C$ of $\Sigma_g\times S^1$, with determinant dual to $\{p\}\times S^1$, where $g\ge2$. Set $S=\mu(\Sigma_g)$ and $P=\mu(x_0)$. Every joint eigenvalue $(\lambda,\nu)$ has $|\lambda|\le2g-2$. Those with equality are exactly $$\label{eq:caps:extreme-spectrum}
       (\lambda,\nu)=\bigl(i^r(2g-2),(-1)^r2\bigr),
                  \qquad r=0,1,2,3.$$ Each corresponding simultaneous generalized eigenspace has dimension one.

On a closed filled piece with $b_1=0$, the real instanton index, complex coupled Dirac index, and real spinor index before phase quotient are $$\label{eq:indices:closed-indices}
 D_I=8\kappa-3(1+b^+),\qquad
 n_D=\Theta-\kappa,\qquad D_{\rm sp}=D_I+2n_D.$$ The pure-line tangential index at type $S$ is $$\label{eq:indices:tangent-index}
 d_s=\frac{K^2-(2\chi+3\sigma)}4.$$

The framed ASD and spinor corrections supplied by this end are $$\label{eq:indices:lens-table}
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
\text{state}&\text{charge congruence}&\delta_I&\delta_{\rm sp}\\ \hline
 +,-&\kappa_N\in\mathbb Z&8\kappa_N&6\kappa_N\\
 \text{trace}&\kappa_N\in\frac14+\mathbb Z
 &2+8(\kappa_N-\frac14)&2+6(\kappa_N-\frac14)
\end{array}$$ For a split main field there are reference fillings with $$\label{eq:indices:reference-fillings}
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
\text{state}&d=v(S)&\kappa_{\rm ref}&\text{choice for an active line}\\ \hline
 +&0&0&K(S)=\Lambda S\\
 -&\pm4&1&|\Lambda S+d|\le4\\
 \text{trace}&\pm2&\frac14&\text{the prescribed boundary eigenline}
\end{array}$$ Here $|\Lambda S|=2$. With these choices the pure-line tangential correction, including stabilizer matching, is zero.

These virtual numbers satisfy $$\label{eq:indices:virtual-sums}
 \sum_i(q_i+4)=4,\qquad
 \sum_i i_i-2\eta=2-4k.$$ For a component of type $R$ or $S$, the split reference fillings of Proposition <a href="#prop:indices:lens-index" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:indices:lens-index">76</a> give $$\label{eq:indices:ell}
 \kappa_i=-\frac{v_i^2}{4}+\ell_i,\qquad \ell_i\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}.$$

For an interior fixed component of type $R$ or $S$ satisfying the incidence property (ii) of Definition <a href="#def:indices:analytic-hypotheses" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="def:indices:analytic-hypotheses">80</a>, $$\label{eq:indices:charge-lower}
 8\kappa_i\ge\max(4s_i-4m_i,\,4m_i),$$ and $$\label{eq:indices:q-score}
 q_i+4\ge
 \max(2s_i+L_i-7m_i,\,m_i+L_i-2s_i).$$ More precisely, if $z_0$ negative-rule assignments have no nonzero half option and $e_0$ positive cells have the exception of Lemma <a href="#lem:indices:positive-square" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:indices:positive-square">82</a>, then $$\label{eq:indices:incidence-charge}
 \ell_i\ge z_0+e_0,\qquad
 8\kappa_i\ge
 4(s_N-z_0)+4m_i-8e_0+8\ell_i
 \ge4s_N+4m_i.$$

For a nonempty contiguous run of $r$ interior components of type $R$, $$\label{eq:indices:mixed-run}
 \sum_{i\text{ in the run}}(4+i_i-p_i)\ge-2.$$ More precisely, let $s,m$ be the total assigned tests and positive cells, let $s_P$ count occupied positive-cell slots, and let $\mathrm{miss}=2m-s_P$. With $\Delta$ the telescoping endpoint bit difference, $$\label{eq:indices:mixed-run-sharp}
 \sum(4+i_i-p_i)
 \ge s-4m+r+\Delta+3\,\mathrm{miss}.$$
